1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Postponed by America's race to the moon.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Nearly defeated by politics.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Almost overcome by technological challenges.

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
How the most complex vehicle ever built soared into space

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:16,000
and fell from the sky.

6
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Now, the triumphs and tragedy of America's first space shuttle.

7
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Columbia, on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,220
January 16, 2003.

9
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:42,140
The seven-member crew of America's 113th Space Shuttle mission

10
00:00:42,141 --> 00:00:46,520
exit the crew quarters on the third floor of the Operations and Checkout Building

11
00:00:46,521 --> 00:00:48,600
at Kennedy Space Center in Florida.

12
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:54,020
They take their traditional walk to the transporter van

13
00:00:54,021 --> 00:00:56,080
that will carry them to the launch pad.

14
00:00:56,081 --> 00:01:03,080
We have booster ignition and liftoff of Space Shuttle Columbia with a multitude of national

15
00:01:03,081 --> 00:01:06,080
and international space research experiments.

16
00:01:06,081 --> 00:01:12,080
They lift off aboard America's first space shuttle, the 22-year-old Columbia.

17
00:01:12,081 --> 00:01:17,080
The astronauts spend the next 16 days in orbit conducting dozens of experiments dedicated

18
00:01:17,081 --> 00:01:20,080
to improving life on Earth.

19
00:01:20,081 --> 00:01:26,080
Finally, each one makes their preparations to re-enter the Earth's atmosphere and return home.

20
00:01:26,081 --> 00:01:30,080
This is amazing. It's really getting fairly bright out there.

21
00:01:30,081 --> 00:01:36,080
Yep. Yeah, you definitely don't want to be outside now. Looks like a blast to us.

22
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:43,080
Only minutes from landing, with friends and families waiting to greet them, disaster strikes.

23
00:01:43,081 --> 00:01:53,080
My fellow Americans, this day has brought terrible news and great sadness to our country.

24
00:01:53,081 --> 00:02:00,080
At 9 o'clock this morning, Mission Control in Houston lost contact with our space shuttle

25
00:02:00,081 --> 00:02:01,080
Columbia.

26
00:02:01,081 --> 00:02:08,080
A short time later, debris was seen falling from the skies above Texas.

27
00:02:08,081 --> 00:02:10,080
The Columbia's lost.

28
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:27,080
Having flown into Columbia, the most difficult emotional part of that for me was looking at my family, my wife and my children, and their emotional response to that

29
00:02:27,326 --> 00:02:28,026
loss.

30
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:36,020
And then the feeling, transferring that feeling to how terrible it must be for the families of the people that were on board the shuttle.

31
00:02:36,021 --> 00:02:39,920
You have a spectacular team that's doing what they can do,

32
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:43,600
but they're trying to manage a vehicle which cannot be made safe.

33
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:47,800
It's a fantastically dangerous and vulnerable and critical machine.

34
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:53,080
With the loss of Columbia, the world is reminded that a successful mission

35
00:02:53,081 --> 00:02:57,100
depends on the performance of millions of parts, systems, and people.

36
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:04,640
With two exceptions, all of America's shuttle missions have met those unwavering conditions.

37
00:03:04,641 --> 00:03:07,660
and Columbia blazed the trail.

38
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:12,200
Soon after NASA was formed in 1958,

39
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:15,700
it began testing precursors to today's space shuttle.

40
00:03:17,660 --> 00:03:21,160
What has motivated people who are interested in spaceflight

41
00:03:21,161 --> 00:03:25,460
from the very beginning is the ability to take off like an airplane,

42
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:28,660
fly into hypersonic and orbital velocities,

43
00:03:29,620 --> 00:03:33,140
go up into orbit, fly in orbit, and come back and land like an airplane.

44
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:33,900
That's what everybody wants.

45
00:03:33,901 --> 00:03:37,060
and to do that repeatedly, just like we do in airliners today.

46
00:03:38,380 --> 00:03:41,700
But by 1961, with the space race accelerating,

47
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:44,800
President John Kennedy had a more immediate goal.

48
00:03:44,801 --> 00:03:48,980
Of landing a man on the moon and returning him safely to the Earth.

49
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:53,140
Beating the Russians to the moon meant using more readily available,

50
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:55,640
single-use-only rockets and spacecraft.

51
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:02,260
But seven years later, NASA's budgets were being cut,

52
00:04:02,261 --> 00:04:04,160
due in part to the Vietnam War.

53
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:09,980
Its future would depend on finding a more economical means of exploring space.

54
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:14,020
So by the summer of 1969,

55
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:19,100
as Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin became the first human beings to explore the moon,

56
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:23,740
plans for a reusable space vehicle had been revived.

57
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:28,660
The concept that NASA really championed

58
00:04:28,661 --> 00:04:33,660
was essentially two fully reusable vehicles.

59
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:37,820
As this early NASA animation illustrates,

60
00:04:37,821 --> 00:04:41,160
the idea was to mate a pair of winged manned spacecraft.

61
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:44,780
The larger, more powerful vehicle

62
00:04:44,781 --> 00:04:47,820
would boost the other to near orbital velocities,

63
00:04:47,821 --> 00:04:51,440
then separate and return to a conventional runway landing

64
00:04:51,441 --> 00:04:52,320
back on Earth.

65
00:04:53,680 --> 00:04:56,260
The smaller vehicle would then fire its own engines

66
00:04:56,261 --> 00:04:57,120
to reach orbit.

67
00:04:57,121 --> 00:05:00,300
The problem with that, it was exceptionally expensive.

68
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:03,700
And the budget constraints said that's not going to really be possible.

69
00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:07,840
Because you would have to develop two full reusable spacecraft.

70
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:11,000
But as plans continued to develop,

71
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:13,740
America was growing more entrenched in Vietnam

72
00:05:13,741 --> 00:05:16,980
and struggling with racial and economic tensions.

73
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:20,360
Many voters and politicians alike

74
00:05:20,361 --> 00:05:23,580
believed the space program should be abandoned altogether

75
00:05:23,581 --> 00:05:26,320
in favor of solving problems at home.

76
00:05:27,120 --> 00:05:32,600
Some of President Richard Nixon's closest advisers, however, convinced him the shuttle must be built.

77
00:05:34,140 --> 00:05:39,560
John Ehrlichman talked about how he had a conversation with Nixon one day in which he says,

78
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:43,900
you know, with this reusable vehicle that can go up into orbit and then return,

79
00:05:44,840 --> 00:05:50,860
we can go up and capture Soviet satellites and bring them back.

80
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:55,340
And Nixon thought that was very cool and wanted to do that.

81
00:05:55,341 --> 00:05:59,500
Now, the shuttle's never been used for that purpose, but that was a selling point.

82
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:03,100
Nixon would hear one other compelling argument as well.

83
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:11,260
Asper Weinberger, who was at that point in time the second person at the Office of Management and Budget in the White House,

84
00:06:11,360 --> 00:06:22,860
made the case in the summer of 1971 that we really needed to approve this new vehicle that NASA was championing, this space shuttle,

85
00:06:22,861 --> 00:06:29,360
because we needed to show that our best days are not behind us,

86
00:06:29,480 --> 00:06:32,820
that we could do something that is for the benefit of all humanity.

87
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:38,380
And he sent a memo into the president in which he made this case,

88
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:41,620
and Nixon wrote across the top of it,

89
00:06:41,720 --> 00:06:42,780
I agree with Cap.

90
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:45,380
It's time to do something about this.

91
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:51,480
On April 21, 1972, Apollo 16 commander John Young

92
00:06:51,481 --> 00:06:55,000
was exploring the moon on NASA's next-to-last lunar landing

93
00:06:55,001 --> 00:06:58,580
when Mission Control transmitted big news from Earth.

94
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:04,820
The House passed a space budget yesterday, 277 to 60,

95
00:07:05,080 --> 00:07:06,660
which includes a vote for the shuttle.

96
00:07:06,920 --> 00:07:08,080
Wonderful! Wonderful!

97
00:07:08,440 --> 00:07:10,200
The country needs that shuttle, my bad.

98
00:07:11,020 --> 00:07:11,720
You'll see.

99
00:07:13,460 --> 00:07:16,200
Congress had just approved funding for the space shuttle.

100
00:07:17,360 --> 00:07:19,760
Young was jubilant, though he couldn't possibly know

101
00:07:19,761 --> 00:07:24,000
that nine years later, he'd serve as commander of the shuttle's maiden space voyage.

102
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:30,400
But before that day came, the technological challenges of building the shuttle

103
00:07:30,401 --> 00:07:32,680
would nearly ground it forever.

104
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:37,400
If the shuttle's three main engines pumped water instead of fuel,

105
00:07:37,880 --> 00:07:41,580
they would drain an average-sized swimming pool every 25 seconds.

106
00:07:46,060 --> 00:07:48,800
When today's astronauts climb aboard the space shuttle,

107
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:53,900
They enter a vehicle designed very much like the one Congress approved back in 1972.

108
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:57,840
The crew lives and works in the orbiter.

109
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:05,640
A combination rocket, spacecraft, and airplane, it is 122 feet long and has a 78-foot wingspan.

110
00:08:06,660 --> 00:08:10,200
The shuttle's three main engines, located at the rear of the orbiter,

111
00:08:10,780 --> 00:08:14,820
are fueled by a million and a half pounds of liquid hydrogen and liquid oxygen,

112
00:08:14,821 --> 00:08:19,100
stored in the 154-foot-tall orange external tank.

113
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:22,380
When the three main engines ignite,

114
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:24,680
it sort of jolts you around a little bit,

115
00:08:24,740 --> 00:08:26,980
kind of like riding a 10-speed down a gravel road

116
00:08:26,981 --> 00:08:27,900
or something like that.

117
00:08:29,360 --> 00:08:33,500
A pair of 149-foot-long solid rocket boosters ignite

118
00:08:33,501 --> 00:08:35,860
along with the main engines at liftoff.

119
00:08:37,160 --> 00:08:40,060
The two boosters produce 44 million horsepower,

120
00:08:41,080 --> 00:08:43,900
the equivalent of nearly 15,000 locomotives.

121
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:48,400
When the solids ignite, it's kind of like riding a dirt bike down a railroad track.

122
00:08:48,440 --> 00:08:49,660
It gives you a pretty good shake.

123
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:55,820
Just over two minutes after liftoff, with the shuttle flying at 3,200 miles per hour,

124
00:08:56,320 --> 00:08:59,240
the boosters burn out and are jettisoned from the vehicle.

125
00:09:00,460 --> 00:09:06,200
There's a small jolt as they are separated from the shuttle, and then they fall back in the ocean.

126
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:12,140
The boosters are recovered, towed back to shore, and refurbished to fly again.

127
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:20,360
52 miles above the Earth, the shuttle's main engines shut down as the external tank runs out of propellant.

128
00:09:21,220 --> 00:09:27,160
The tank then separates from the orbiter and breaks up as it falls back through the intense heat of the Earth's atmosphere.

129
00:09:28,240 --> 00:09:30,460
It is the only expendable part of the shuttle.

130
00:09:32,200 --> 00:09:37,220
The pilot then activates a set of smaller, liquid-fueled maneuvering engines to reach orbit.

131
00:09:38,220 --> 00:09:41,860
Eight minutes after liftoff, the orbiter is circling the Earth.

132
00:09:41,861 --> 00:09:44,900
at 17,500 miles per hour.

133
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:51,960
Returning home, it endures temperatures up to 2,900 degrees Fahrenheit

134
00:09:51,961 --> 00:09:54,040
as it re-enters the Earth's atmosphere.

135
00:09:56,100 --> 00:10:00,160
The orbiter must then glide, completely unpowered by engines,

136
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:04,700
from halfway around the world to touch down on a specific runway.

137
00:10:05,900 --> 00:10:09,140
Such performance is still spectacular by today's standards,

138
00:10:09,141 --> 00:10:14,040
But the space shuttle was designed and built in the early 1970s,

139
00:10:14,480 --> 00:10:17,420
years before Americans enjoyed personal computers,

140
00:10:18,020 --> 00:10:21,460
digital display clocks, or even telephone answering machines.

141
00:10:22,220 --> 00:10:25,060
Building the shuttle was just as complicated

142
00:10:25,061 --> 00:10:27,300
as going to the moon in many respects.

143
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:29,980
It required technologies that didn't exist.

144
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:33,200
In some cases, NASA engineers didn't quite know

145
00:10:33,201 --> 00:10:34,600
how to get to those technologies.

146
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:36,900
They could kind of roughly define what they needed,

147
00:10:36,901 --> 00:10:38,320
but they weren't sure how to get there.

148
00:10:39,140 --> 00:10:44,140
Developing those technologies would involve the contributions of over 50,000 Americans

149
00:10:44,141 --> 00:10:49,140
working for hundreds of contractors in 47 different states.

150
00:10:49,141 --> 00:10:58,140
The solid rocket boosters were assembled, loaded with propellants, and tested by the Thiokol Company in Brigham City, Utah.

151
00:10:58,141 --> 00:11:06,140
They're made from weld-free, high-strength steel in a process called rolled ring forging.

152
00:11:06,141 --> 00:11:13,140
Each booster contains 11 segments that are joined together using high-strength steel pins.

153
00:11:13,141 --> 00:11:19,140
In New Orleans, Martin Marietta was at work on the huge external tank.

154
00:11:19,141 --> 00:11:28,140
Since the temperature of liquid hydrogen is 423 degrees below zero, the tank would be encased in spray-on insulation.

155
00:11:28,141 --> 00:11:35,140
This would prevent ice from accumulating on its surface in the humidity of its Florida launch site.

156
00:11:35,141 --> 00:11:42,920
The most complex job went to Rockwell International in Southern California.

157
00:11:42,921 --> 00:11:47,200
Rockwell would design and assemble the orbiter itself.

158
00:11:47,201 --> 00:11:52,420
Its Rocketdyne division was responsible for the shuttle's main engines.

159
00:11:52,421 --> 00:11:58,440
In all, the shuttle would consist of 2.5 million parts, including more than a thousand plumbing

160
00:11:58,441 --> 00:12:04,280
valves and connections, 1,400 circuit breakers, and 230 miles of wire.

161
00:12:05,420 --> 00:12:11,780
I remember when I started on the program, I asked myself, is it possible to do this thing?

162
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:13,637
Is it really possible?

163
00:12:13,638 --> 00:12:16,560
Because when you looked at all the things that have to happen,

164
00:12:17,240 --> 00:12:21,540
and the time was allocated, emotionally you thought, or intellectually you thought,

165
00:12:21,580 --> 00:12:22,981
it's just not possible.

166
00:12:22,982 --> 00:12:26,940
But what we did is instead we just focused on the year at a time.

167
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:35,560
On the morning of September 17, 1976, the Rockwell team treated the world to a glimpse of the future.

168
00:12:36,840 --> 00:12:43,060
A test model of the orbiter, one not intended for spaceflight, rolled out of their Palmdale, California plant.

169
00:12:44,080 --> 00:12:49,000
It was named Enterprise, after the starship in the television series Star Trek.

170
00:12:51,400 --> 00:12:58,180
In August of 1977, astronauts Fred Hayes and Gordon Fullerton would put Enterprise to the test.

171
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:07,440
Their job was to release the 150,000-pound glider from the back of a specially equipped 747 jumbo jet,

172
00:13:07,441 --> 00:13:12,440
and with only one chance for success, attempt a landing.

173
00:13:12,441 --> 00:13:18,440
At 24,000 feet, Hayes fired the explosive bolts holding the aircraft together,

174
00:13:18,441 --> 00:13:23,440
and as expected, Enterprise lifted straight up as the jetliner veered away.

175
00:13:23,441 --> 00:13:29,880
Five minutes later, Hayes glided Enterprise toward the runway for what would be a perfect

176
00:13:29,881 --> 00:13:31,920
landing.

177
00:13:31,921 --> 00:13:36,680
But the test only proved the shuttle as a glider, not as a spacecraft.

178
00:13:36,681 --> 00:13:41,720
That test would be up to the shuttle Columbia, which was nearing completion but was plagued

179
00:13:41,721 --> 00:13:45,340
with two major problems.

180
00:13:45,341 --> 00:13:52,440
The first involved the shuttle's three main engines, the most complex engines ever developed.

181
00:13:52,441 --> 00:13:59,540
One of the difficult new requirements for this rocket engine is that it be reusable.

182
00:13:59,541 --> 00:14:03,360
In fact, it's to be reused for 55 flights.

183
00:14:03,361 --> 00:14:09,560
Previously, all rocket engines were expendable.

184
00:14:09,561 --> 00:14:14,300
In addition, the engines needed to be much lighter than prior rocket engines.

185
00:14:14,301 --> 00:14:19,940
Yet for their weight, they had to be far more powerful, capable of generating 23 times as

186
00:14:19,941 --> 00:14:22,420
much power as Hoover Dam.

187
00:14:22,421 --> 00:14:27,180
The individual turbine blades were roughly the size of a postage stamp,

188
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:30,640
and it developed 600 horsepower per blade.

189
00:14:31,900 --> 00:14:35,100
During testing, those turbine blades cracked,

190
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:38,580
and nozzles gave out under the extreme pressures and vibrations.

191
00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:44,380
It was later discovered that the grade of welding wire used on the engines

192
00:14:44,381 --> 00:14:46,980
was causing weaknesses in welded areas.

193
00:14:48,820 --> 00:14:51,620
By upgrading the wire and making other modifications,

194
00:14:51,621 --> 00:14:54,620
the engines began testing with consistent success.

195
00:14:54,621 --> 00:14:59,620
The second challenge that they had was a thermal protection system,

196
00:14:59,621 --> 00:15:02,620
or the TPS as it's called.

197
00:15:02,621 --> 00:15:07,620
The TPS protects the orbiter from burning up as it re-enters the atmosphere.

198
00:15:07,621 --> 00:15:09,620
It would consist of three materials.

199
00:15:09,621 --> 00:15:14,620
Reinforced carbon-carbon panels were installed on the nose and wing edges.

200
00:15:14,621 --> 00:15:18,620
The sections that encountered the highest temperatures.

201
00:15:18,621 --> 00:15:25,620
Most of the top side of the orbiter was considered least vulnerable and covered with a flame-resistant fabric.

202
00:15:25,621 --> 00:15:34,620
The greatest challenge was protecting the largest section of the orbiter, especially the underside, which dissipates most of the heat.

203
00:15:34,621 --> 00:15:45,620
These areas would be protected by nearly 31,000 individually designed, lightweight, heat-resistant, ceramic tiles.

204
00:15:45,621 --> 00:15:50,000
The problem was getting the tiles to bond to the orbiter's aluminum skin.

205
00:15:51,560 --> 00:15:52,660
This tile is a ceramic.

206
00:15:53,660 --> 00:15:58,600
And if you really looked under a microscope, it's probably 97% air.

207
00:15:59,320 --> 00:16:00,020
There's nothing there.

208
00:16:00,660 --> 00:16:06,720
It's sand in strands that have been heat treated and then a coating put on.

209
00:16:06,721 --> 00:16:10,340
And when we put that tile attached to the vehicle,

210
00:16:10,780 --> 00:16:15,360
it was breaking at a point where we didn't understand why it was breaking.

211
00:16:15,620 --> 00:16:22,620
In March 1979, a blue ribbon panel of experts from around the country began searching for

212
00:16:22,621 --> 00:16:24,620
a solution to the tile problem.

213
00:16:24,621 --> 00:16:30,620
At the same time, in order to maintain a processing schedule, NASA transported Columbia to the

214
00:16:30,621 --> 00:16:35,620
Kennedy Space Center, with thousands of tiles still missing.

215
00:16:35,621 --> 00:16:40,620
Six months later, the experts determined that the solution was to add a special coating

216
00:16:40,621 --> 00:16:45,180
a special coating to the back of each tile, strengthening where it bonds to the orbiter.

217
00:16:47,340 --> 00:16:53,100
Workers at the Kennedy Space Center would spend until Christmas 1980 modifying, applying,

218
00:16:53,101 --> 00:16:57,740
and waterproofing the 30,759 tiles on the orbiter.

219
00:17:00,540 --> 00:17:07,900
By the spring of 1981, Columbia, the most complex and expensive vehicle ever built, was ready to fly.

220
00:17:10,620 --> 00:17:17,599
Columbia was named for a Boston sailing vessel captained by Robert Gray, who in 1790 completed the first U.S.

221
00:17:17,600 --> 00:17:19,520
circumnavigation of the globe.

222
00:17:23,580 --> 00:17:32,100
Prior to every space shuttle flight, a series of events take place at Florida's Kennedy Space Center that is nearly as spectacular as liftoff itself.

223
00:17:32,101 --> 00:17:40,160
The shuttle is rolled out of its processing hangar on a 96-wheel transporter and carried

224
00:17:40,161 --> 00:17:45,640
to the 525-foot-tall Vehicle Assembly Building, which was originally built to assemble Saturn

225
00:17:45,641 --> 00:17:50,820
V rockets during the Apollo era.

226
00:17:50,821 --> 00:17:55,340
The newly completed Columbia made this trip for the first time in November 1980.

227
00:17:55,341 --> 00:18:01,620
The first time Columbia rolled out of the processing facility after years of working

228
00:18:01,621 --> 00:18:06,640
there and rolled over the VAB, there were thousands of people out there just to watch

229
00:18:06,641 --> 00:18:09,757
that short trip between the processing facility and the VAB.

230
00:18:09,758 --> 00:18:11,420
All those technicians that glued

231
00:18:11,421 --> 00:18:16,040
tile on, all those engineers that wrote software, all those people that worked in logistics that

232
00:18:16,041 --> 00:18:21,260
procured parts, everybody felt that they were a member of the team.

233
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:27,860
Waiting for every orbiter inside the vehicle assembly building are the solid rocket boosters

234
00:18:27,861 --> 00:18:33,340
and external tank, which have been raised to the vertical position and attached together.

235
00:18:34,960 --> 00:18:38,940
A pair of cranes lift the orbiter vertically toward the top of the building,

236
00:18:39,460 --> 00:18:40,920
high above the external tank.

237
00:18:42,460 --> 00:18:46,880
It's aligned with the tank, then is gently lowered down and attached to it.

238
00:18:48,920 --> 00:18:54,740
Next, this slow-moving 6 million-pound vehicle called the Shuttle Crawler Transporter

239
00:18:54,741 --> 00:18:58,980
carries the spacecraft out of the building and toward the launch pad.

240
00:19:01,060 --> 00:19:05,000
Fully loaded, the crawler moves at one mile per hour.

241
00:19:06,420 --> 00:19:09,600
For its maiden voyage, mission STS-1,

242
00:19:10,360 --> 00:19:13,640
Columbia arrived at the pad on December 31, 1980,

243
00:19:14,460 --> 00:19:18,120
four months prior to launch, for a series of pre-launch tests.

244
00:19:18,121 --> 00:19:25,100
The spacecraft was nothing like we had ever checked out or launched before.

245
00:19:25,320 --> 00:19:33,680
Most of the checkout with the previous capsules or spacecraft were switches in the cockpit and manual activity.

246
00:19:34,560 --> 00:19:44,380
This brought in a computer complex on board and drove us to more computers in our ground checkout than we'd ever had before.

247
00:19:44,381 --> 00:19:48,520
So our engineers had become very proficient in computers,

248
00:19:48,740 --> 00:19:54,400
and for people that had grown up in the slide rule era, this was a challenge.

249
00:19:56,580 --> 00:20:00,260
The computers would play a key role in firing the shuttle's engines

250
00:20:00,261 --> 00:20:02,160
and igniting the solid rocket boosters.

251
00:20:03,100 --> 00:20:08,900
The Air Force was telling Congress that the shuttle will lose one out of 30 missions

252
00:20:08,901 --> 00:20:11,720
due to solid rocket boosters alone.

253
00:20:12,720 --> 00:20:14,940
They said, we're sorry to give you the news,

254
00:20:15,440 --> 00:20:18,100
but we are operating solid rocket boosters,

255
00:20:18,620 --> 00:20:19,740
and that's our record.

256
00:20:21,240 --> 00:20:24,060
So SDS-1 had a lot of unknowns.

257
00:20:24,900 --> 00:20:26,060
There was a lot of risk taken.

258
00:20:26,140 --> 00:20:29,300
The people who flew that really were quite courageous

259
00:20:29,301 --> 00:20:30,800
because there was no guarantee.

260
00:20:32,140 --> 00:20:35,520
Those people would include NASA's most experienced astronaut,

261
00:20:36,340 --> 00:20:39,580
John Young, a veteran of two Gemini missions

262
00:20:39,581 --> 00:20:41,140
as well as two Apollo missions.

263
00:20:41,720 --> 00:20:45,400
was selected to command Columbia.

264
00:20:45,401 --> 00:20:47,740
The pilot was Bob Crippen.

265
00:20:47,741 --> 00:20:49,700
Though he was an Apollo-era astronaut

266
00:20:49,701 --> 00:20:53,740
and had more than 4,000 hours of jet aircraft flying time,

267
00:20:53,741 --> 00:20:58,280
this would be his first space flight.

268
00:20:58,281 --> 00:21:02,240
On April 12, 1981, Young and Crippen posed for cameras

269
00:21:02,241 --> 00:21:07,140
during the astronauts' traditional pre-launch breakfast.

270
00:21:07,141 --> 00:21:09,220
In a few hours, they would be flying

271
00:21:09,221 --> 00:21:11,180
the first vehicle sent into space

272
00:21:11,181 --> 00:21:14,180
without the benefit of previously unmanned orbital testing.

273
00:21:16,380 --> 00:21:18,540
It was almost like Apollo again.

274
00:21:18,600 --> 00:21:19,660
We hadn't been in space.

275
00:21:20,240 --> 00:21:21,920
In 1981, when they flew Columbia,

276
00:21:22,060 --> 00:21:26,400
we had not sent humans into space since 1975, six-year absence.

277
00:21:26,740 --> 00:21:28,520
So everybody was excited about this,

278
00:21:28,820 --> 00:21:31,280
and then there were all these unknowns about this mission.

279
00:21:31,760 --> 00:21:33,220
Would the engine work properly?

280
00:21:33,221 --> 00:21:36,760
These shuttle tiles that were glued onto the underside of the orbiter,

281
00:21:36,880 --> 00:21:38,180
would that be successful?

282
00:21:38,840 --> 00:21:40,640
We've gone for main engine start.

283
00:21:40,641 --> 00:21:42,220
We have main engine start.

284
00:21:46,300 --> 00:21:47,620
We have a red flag.

285
00:21:47,880 --> 00:21:50,260
It's from America's first space shuttle.

286
00:21:50,720 --> 00:21:52,980
And the shuttle has cleared the tower.

287
00:21:55,720 --> 00:21:57,780
As Columbia made its ascent,

288
00:21:58,800 --> 00:22:01,560
Crippen and Young noticed debris flying past their window.

289
00:22:02,820 --> 00:22:06,320
It would later be determined that this was ice and insulation

290
00:22:06,321 --> 00:22:07,540
from the external tank,

291
00:22:07,541 --> 00:22:11,340
an occurrence that would become common on future flights

292
00:22:11,341 --> 00:22:15,840
and prove fatal on Columbia's final flight.

293
00:22:16,900 --> 00:22:18,180
We see SRV set flight.

294
00:22:19,400 --> 00:22:20,200
Roger on a set.

295
00:22:23,440 --> 00:22:25,680
The rest of the ascent went flawlessly.

296
00:22:26,740 --> 00:22:29,420
Once in orbit, Young and Crippen's first test

297
00:22:29,421 --> 00:22:32,460
would be opening the 60-foot-long payload bay doors.

298
00:22:33,320 --> 00:22:35,580
But this procedure was more than a test.

299
00:22:35,581 --> 00:22:38,580
Okay, the fourth door is coming open now.

300
00:22:38,581 --> 00:22:40,580
Roger, copy.

301
00:22:40,581 --> 00:22:44,580
Inside the doors are radiators that help cool the shuttle,

302
00:22:44,581 --> 00:22:49,580
so opening them as soon as possible would be a critical step in all shuttle missions.

303
00:22:49,581 --> 00:22:53,580
The doors opened perfectly, but when they did,

304
00:22:53,581 --> 00:22:56,580
Young and Crippen pointed cameras back toward the engine pods

305
00:22:56,581 --> 00:22:59,580
and saw a disturbing sight.

306
00:22:59,581 --> 00:23:04,640
We do have a few tiles missing off of both of them.

307
00:23:05,400 --> 00:23:10,280
Off of the starboard pod, three tile and some smaller pieces.

308
00:23:10,900 --> 00:23:17,960
And off the port pod, looks like I see one full square and looks like a few little triangular shapes.

309
00:23:19,340 --> 00:23:24,180
NASA determined that since these were the less critical white tiles on the top side of the orbiter,

310
00:23:24,720 --> 00:23:27,040
as opposed to the black tiles on the underside,

311
00:23:27,041 --> 00:23:34,660
A safe re-entry was still possible, assuming the tiles on the underside were not damaged

312
00:23:34,661 --> 00:23:37,020
as well.

313
00:23:37,021 --> 00:23:41,740
Nobody knew with certainty whether they were or not.

314
00:23:41,741 --> 00:23:46,920
Two days later, Columbia attempted to become the first winged spacecraft to re-enter the

315
00:23:46,921 --> 00:23:49,920
atmosphere and make a conventional runway landing.

316
00:23:49,921 --> 00:23:54,920
John, we're all riding with you.

317
00:23:54,921 --> 00:24:00,280
Back on Earth, a six-mile-long string of traffic was carrying half a million spectators

318
00:24:00,281 --> 00:24:06,040
to Edwards Air Force Base in California to witness the historic touchdown.

319
00:24:06,041 --> 00:24:10,680
While Columbia was enduring the searing heat of reentry, Mission Control would lose radio

320
00:24:10,681 --> 00:24:13,200
contact for approximately 20 minutes.

321
00:24:13,201 --> 00:24:18,240
And they lost it just as they were supposed to, and as you come out of that, they weren't

322
00:24:18,241 --> 00:24:21,200
sure that they would hear these guys again.

323
00:24:21,201 --> 00:24:26,480
concerned that they knew some of those shuttle tiles had been popped off during the launch.

324
00:24:27,180 --> 00:24:30,820
Well, what would happen? Would there be a burn-through of these individual places?

325
00:24:31,160 --> 00:24:32,720
Would the crew come back safely?

326
00:24:36,720 --> 00:24:38,340
What a way to come to California.

327
00:24:39,280 --> 00:24:40,997
They regain the telemetry.

328
00:24:40,998 --> 00:24:44,960
They hear a sonic boom as it comes below the speed of sound.

329
00:24:45,220 --> 00:24:50,300
They see it off in the distance, and Walter Cronkite literally breathes a sigh of relief,

330
00:24:50,301 --> 00:24:53,140
wipes his brow and says, they've made it.

331
00:24:57,540 --> 00:25:03,020
Soon after landing, John Young was supposed to remain on board for a brief medical exam.

332
00:25:04,940 --> 00:25:08,300
Instead, he came bounding out of the shuttle ahead of time.

333
00:25:09,460 --> 00:25:11,940
In a post-flight statement, he explained why.

334
00:25:12,280 --> 00:25:17,600
Everyone had said that they had shown, from an engineering statistics standpoint,

335
00:25:17,601 --> 00:25:21,200
that it was impossible, impossible, mind you,

336
00:25:21,500 --> 00:25:25,240
that some of those critical tiles on the bottom of the vehicle would not fall off,

337
00:25:25,500 --> 00:25:27,040
or none of those tiles fell off.

338
00:25:27,400 --> 00:25:30,200
And it's all due to the individual effort of the American people.

339
00:25:32,940 --> 00:25:37,500
Over the next 15 months, Columbia would fly three more two-man missions

340
00:25:37,501 --> 00:25:40,640
to test the shuttle's various systems in different orbits.

341
00:25:43,160 --> 00:25:46,640
Its fifth flight launched November 11, 1982,

342
00:25:46,641 --> 00:25:50,520
was considered the space shuttle's first fully operational mission,

343
00:25:51,240 --> 00:25:54,960
and marked the first time a four-man crew ever flew into space.

344
00:25:56,540 --> 00:25:59,680
Columbia would soon be joined by three sister orbiters.

345
00:26:00,780 --> 00:26:03,880
Together, they would make space travel seem routine,

346
00:26:04,820 --> 00:26:06,640
until disaster proved otherwise.

347
00:26:08,480 --> 00:26:11,140
Columbia's maiden voyage was made on the 20th anniversary

348
00:26:11,141 --> 00:26:15,540
of the first manned spaceflight by Soviet cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin.

349
00:26:16,640 --> 00:26:26,640
During the first 22 years of the space shuttle program, few astronauts spent more hours in orbit than Story Musgrave.

350
00:26:26,641 --> 00:26:30,640
His sixth and final mission was on board the Columbia.

351
00:26:30,641 --> 00:26:37,640
Launched in November of 1996, the mission distinguished him as the only person to ever fly on all five shuttles.

352
00:26:37,641 --> 00:26:43,640
shuttles, and at age 61, he became the oldest person to ever fly in space, until

353
00:26:43,641 --> 00:26:51,560
John Glenn flew on the shuttle Discovery in 1998 at age 77. But to this astronaut,

354
00:26:51,561 --> 00:26:56,400
who joined NASA during the Apollo era and played key roles in developing

355
00:26:56,401 --> 00:27:02,600
several shuttle systems, flying the reusable spacecraft was anything but

356
00:27:02,601 --> 00:27:10,815
routine. The vehicle that we were getting into, it was simply frightening.

357
00:27:10,816 --> 00:27:12,480
I don't do this

358
00:27:12,481 --> 00:27:15,786
because it's dangerous and I don't do it because it's risky.

359
00:27:15,787 --> 00:27:17,660
I do it because I'm a space person

360
00:27:17,661 --> 00:27:19,740
and I have no other way to get up there.

361
00:27:19,741 --> 00:27:22,080
I certainly would have rather gotten there by

362
00:27:22,081 --> 00:27:23,996
Mercury, Gemini or Apollo.

363
00:27:23,997 --> 00:27:29,300
Vehicles that are 10 times safer or 100 times safer than the shuttle is.

364
00:27:29,301 --> 00:27:35,540
Yet throughout the early 1980s, the shuttle would continue with one successful mission

365
00:27:35,541 --> 00:27:36,540
after the next.

366
00:27:36,541 --> 00:27:44,240
Way out there on the end of the runway, the space shuttle Challenger, affixed atop a 747,

367
00:27:44,241 --> 00:27:49,200
is about to start on the first leg of a journey that will eventually put it into space in

368
00:27:49,201 --> 00:27:50,200
November.

369
00:27:50,201 --> 00:27:53,560
It's headed for Florida now.

370
00:27:53,561 --> 00:28:03,400
With the addition of three more orbiters, Challenger in 1983, Discovery in 1984, and Atlantis in 1985,

371
00:28:03,401 --> 00:28:07,840
NASA was pushing to launch shuttles as quickly as possible.

372
00:28:07,841 --> 00:28:10,600
Turn them around like airplanes initially was the thought.

373
00:28:10,601 --> 00:28:14,440
They found out very quickly that wasn't possible, but we could still fly,

374
00:28:14,441 --> 00:28:18,960
maybe as many as 25 missions a year, you know, one every other week.

375
00:28:18,961 --> 00:28:21,920
And that was a very exciting prospect.

376
00:28:21,921 --> 00:28:23,520
And to get there, you have to push very hard.

377
00:28:23,860 --> 00:28:29,400
And so all through 1985, the NASA Administrator is pressuring the shuttle program to launch on schedule.

378
00:28:29,401 --> 00:28:34,040
And if you look at the number of launches that year, they're much greater than in any other year in the history of the program.

379
00:28:35,420 --> 00:28:42,220
1986 was expected to be equally as demanding, and Columbia was scheduled to fly the year's first mission in January.

380
00:28:43,840 --> 00:28:50,180
Despite pressure to launch, the mission set a record of seven postponements, four with the crew on board,

381
00:28:50,181 --> 00:28:53,320
which included U.S. Congressman Bill Nelson of Florida.

382
00:28:53,820 --> 00:29:02,340
Only after the fact did I find out some of the near misses that we had in those four scrubs.

383
00:29:02,560 --> 00:29:05,420
Everything is still. The orbiters on board computers have armed the SLB.

384
00:29:05,460 --> 00:29:06,160
We have a cutoff.

385
00:29:06,260 --> 00:29:07,080
We have a cutoff.

386
00:29:08,400 --> 00:29:11,460
One scrub occurred just 31 seconds to launch,

387
00:29:11,840 --> 00:29:13,860
when it was discovered that a fatigued technician

388
00:29:13,861 --> 00:29:20,080
had accidentally allowed 18,000 pounds of liquid oxygen to drain out of the external tank.

389
00:29:20,180 --> 00:29:28,180
If we had launched 31 seconds later, we would have not had enough fuel to get to orbital

390
00:29:28,181 --> 00:29:35,340
velocity and therefore Hoot Gibson, our commander, would have had to put down a fully loaded

391
00:29:35,341 --> 00:29:43,140
spacecraft in an emergency landing on a 10,000 foot runway in Dakar, Senegal where the end

392
00:29:43,141 --> 00:29:48,800
of the runway dropped straight off down a cliff into the Atlantic Ocean.

393
00:29:48,801 --> 00:29:53,529
finally lifted off on January 12, 1986 for a six-day mission.

394
00:29:53,530 --> 00:29:55,700
The space shuttle Challenger

395
00:29:55,701 --> 00:30:01,000
with teacher Krista McAuliffe was set to launch just four days after Columbia returned home.

396
00:30:01,360 --> 00:30:07,405
I don't think any teacher has ever been more ready to have two lessons in my life.

397
00:30:07,406 --> 00:30:08,106
I've

398
00:30:08,107 --> 00:30:11,638
been preparing these since September, and I just hope everybody tunes in.

399
00:30:11,639 --> 00:30:13,040
Krista McAuliffe was going

400
00:30:13,041 --> 00:30:16,800
to do this broadcast from space, this lesson from space.

401
00:30:16,801 --> 00:30:24,340
It was going to be, go literally around the world and be taught to students all over.

402
00:30:24,341 --> 00:30:29,720
Never before had two shuttle missions been scheduled to launch so close to each other.

403
00:30:29,721 --> 00:30:35,160
Weather and mechanical delays, however, pushed the Challenger launch back six days to January

404
00:30:35,161 --> 00:30:37,380
28th.

405
00:30:37,381 --> 00:30:43,560
On that day, the temperature was a frigid 36 degrees, 15 degrees colder than any prior

406
00:30:43,561 --> 00:30:49,620
launch, and icicles could be seen forming on the launch pad.

407
00:30:49,621 --> 00:30:54,100
While the crew was having its pre-launch breakfast, engineers who had designed the solid rocket

408
00:30:54,101 --> 00:30:59,500
boosters were warning NASA that such temperatures could trigger a catastrophic failure in the

409
00:30:59,501 --> 00:31:01,500
booster's joint seals.

410
00:31:01,501 --> 00:31:08,500
The technical decision in those matters has got to be not just analytical data,

411
00:31:08,660 --> 00:31:10,920
but it has to be common sense data.

412
00:31:11,220 --> 00:31:14,160
Like foot and a half long icicles on the launch pad,

413
00:31:14,300 --> 00:31:18,060
you look at the icicles and you say, no, what is this?

414
00:31:18,520 --> 00:31:19,360
What are we doing?

415
00:31:20,340 --> 00:31:21,300
And you don't go.

416
00:31:23,380 --> 00:31:25,020
But the shuttle did launch,

417
00:31:25,660 --> 00:31:28,520
and with schoolchildren the world over watching on television,

418
00:31:28,521 --> 00:31:32,060
The worst accident in the history of U.S.

419
00:31:32,061 --> 00:31:35,860
space exploration occurred 73 seconds later.

420
00:31:40,280 --> 00:31:44,580
The cause of the accident was blamed on a design flaw in the solid rocket boosters

421
00:31:44,581 --> 00:31:49,620
and on NASA management for putting money and expediency ahead of safety.

422
00:31:51,700 --> 00:31:55,580
The Challenger tragedy grounded the space shuttle for the next 32 months.

423
00:31:56,480 --> 00:32:01,740
Columbia and the two remaining orbiters were transported back to their California manufacturing facility

424
00:32:01,741 --> 00:32:05,080
for more than 200 safety improvements and modifications.

425
00:32:07,860 --> 00:32:10,900
There was a major redesign of the solid rocket boosters,

426
00:32:11,420 --> 00:32:13,740
and improvements were made to the shuttle's main engines.

427
00:32:16,640 --> 00:32:19,840
NASA also commissioned the building of the space shuttle Endeavour,

428
00:32:20,380 --> 00:32:22,820
which would make its maiden voyage in 1992.

429
00:32:22,821 --> 00:32:30,820
But perhaps more significantly, NASA's management structure, safety, and decision-making procedures

430
00:32:30,821 --> 00:32:32,820
were drastically overhauled.

431
00:32:32,821 --> 00:32:38,820
It put control of technical operations in the hands of the technicians again, and they

432
00:32:38,821 --> 00:32:42,865
would resist any interference by people that didn't know the technology.

433
00:32:42,866 --> 00:32:43,820
And so the Launch

434
00:32:43,821 --> 00:32:47,820
Control Director was responsible for launches, the Mission Control was responsible for running

435
00:32:47,821 --> 00:32:49,446
missions, and that's the way it went.

436
00:32:49,447 --> 00:32:51,820
And I've been incredibly proud of that team, confident

437
00:32:51,821 --> 00:32:54,220
confident of that team.

438
00:32:54,221 --> 00:32:58,480
Throughout the 1990s, Columbia flew 16 missions,

439
00:32:58,481 --> 00:33:01,520
launching satellites, conducting hundreds of experiments

440
00:33:01,521 --> 00:33:05,100
to improve life on Earth, and helping us better understand

441
00:33:05,101 --> 00:33:08,200
the depth of our universe.

442
00:33:08,201 --> 00:33:12,540
On March 1, 2002, Columbia flew to the Hubble telescope

443
00:33:12,541 --> 00:33:16,380
to pay a service call.

444
00:33:16,381 --> 00:33:19,040
Among other improvements, the crew replaced a camera

445
00:33:19,041 --> 00:33:21,500
with one that had twice the field of view

446
00:33:21,501 --> 00:33:23,200
and five times the resolution.

447
00:33:24,400 --> 00:33:26,220
Meanwhile, back on Earth,

448
00:33:26,221 --> 00:33:28,880
the crew for Columbia's next voyage was busy training

449
00:33:28,881 --> 00:33:31,660
for one of the largest science missions ever flown.

450
00:33:32,660 --> 00:33:35,140
It would prove to be Columbia's final journey.

451
00:33:36,460 --> 00:33:39,280
In June 1995, NASA delayed the launch

452
00:33:39,281 --> 00:33:41,440
of the shuttle Discovery after finding

453
00:33:41,441 --> 00:33:43,540
that woodpeckers had poked dozens of holes

454
00:33:43,541 --> 00:33:45,860
in the external fuel tank's foam insulation.

455
00:33:45,861 --> 00:33:51,860
America's shuttle program opened space travel to the world.

456
00:33:53,020 --> 00:33:58,640
Colombia alone hosted astronauts from Germany, Canada, Italy, Ukraine, and Japan.

457
00:34:04,520 --> 00:34:11,360
In 1998, the first astronaut from Israel moved his family to Houston to begin training to fly on Colombia.

458
00:34:11,361 --> 00:34:19,300
Ilan Ramon, married with four children, was the son of a Holocaust survivor and a fighter

459
00:34:19,301 --> 00:34:22,360
pilot in the Israeli Air Force.

460
00:34:22,361 --> 00:34:25,660
His crewmates would become like a second family.

461
00:34:25,661 --> 00:34:31,200
They were Commander Rick Husband, married with two young children.

462
00:34:31,201 --> 00:34:36,200
Columbia would be the second shuttle flight for this Air Force test pilot.

463
00:34:36,201 --> 00:34:39,620
Pilot Willie McCool, married with three children.

464
00:34:39,621 --> 00:34:45,300
He was a Navy fighter pilot who hadn't yet flown a shuttle mission.

465
00:34:45,301 --> 00:34:49,240
Payload Commander Michael Anderson, married with two children.

466
00:34:49,241 --> 00:34:53,880
This Air Force pilot had flown one prior shuttle mission.

467
00:34:53,881 --> 00:34:58,560
Mission Specialist Kalpana Chawla, or KC to the rest of the group.

468
00:34:58,561 --> 00:35:03,520
Married with no children, this aeronautical engineer and pilot was born in India and flew

469
00:35:03,521 --> 00:35:07,540
one previous shuttle mission.

470
00:35:07,541 --> 00:35:13,540
Mission Specialist David Brown, the only bachelor on the crew, was a physician, Navy pilot,

471
00:35:13,541 --> 00:35:18,620
and flight surgeon, as well as a former circus performer.

472
00:35:18,621 --> 00:35:22,880
Columbia would be his first shuttle flight.

473
00:35:22,881 --> 00:35:28,540
Mission Specialist Laurel Clark, married with one child, she was also a pediatrician, a

474
00:35:28,541 --> 00:35:31,780
flight surgeon, and a Navy commander.

475
00:35:31,781 --> 00:35:34,300
This would be her first shuttle mission as well.

476
00:35:34,301 --> 00:35:38,640
Well, all the crew members were excited about being on the science mission,

477
00:35:38,641 --> 00:35:41,040
and that's true for any mission that flies in space.

478
00:35:41,120 --> 00:35:42,520
The crew's always excited to be there.

479
00:35:43,020 --> 00:35:44,580
But this one perhaps even more so,

480
00:35:44,660 --> 00:35:48,140
because it's one of the few that was purely dedicated to science.

481
00:35:49,080 --> 00:35:53,400
The crew's 16-day mission would require them to complete more than 80 experiments.

482
00:35:55,020 --> 00:35:59,580
They were designed to shed light on such areas as combating prostate and bone cancer,

483
00:35:59,581 --> 00:36:04,660
kidney stone formation, sleep patterns, and fire suppression.

484
00:36:07,180 --> 00:36:13,388
With a huge workload ahead of them, the excited crew lifted off at 10.39 a.m.

485
00:36:13,389 --> 00:36:15,000
on January 16, 2003.

486
00:36:17,020 --> 00:36:23,000
By initial appearances, the launch in ascent seemed normal, and Columbia reached its planned orbit.

487
00:36:24,860 --> 00:36:29,240
The following day, however, NASA engineers reviewed video of the launch,

488
00:36:29,580 --> 00:36:41,513
They discovered that 81 seconds after liftoff, at 60,000 feet, a chunk of foam from the external tank struck the protective carbon-carbon panel on the edge of the left

489
00:36:41,514 --> 00:36:46,800
wing, an area where temperatures reached nearly 3,000 degrees on re-entry.

490
00:36:48,260 --> 00:36:55,500
Since Columbia's first flight, NASA was aware of foam striking the orbiter, but it had been dismissed as a serious safety threat.

491
00:36:55,501 --> 00:36:59,500
Yet this strike did concern some engineers.

492
00:36:59,501 --> 00:37:04,500
Though lightweight, the object was estimated to be three feet long

493
00:37:04,501 --> 00:37:08,500
and struck when the shuttle's speed was about 500 miles per hour.

494
00:37:08,501 --> 00:37:10,185
You know what a milk carton is?

495
00:37:10,186 --> 00:37:13,500
Try something that weighs one and a half times a milk carton,

496
00:37:13,501 --> 00:37:18,500
it's in your face at 500 miles an hour. It's going to take your head off.

497
00:37:18,501 --> 00:37:21,500
While the crew was going about its work in orbit,

498
00:37:22,260 --> 00:37:27,280
NASA engineers had issued reports indicating the strike could have caused a breach on the edge of the wing,

499
00:37:27,820 --> 00:37:29,500
which would doom the shuttle on re-entry.

500
00:37:31,960 --> 00:37:35,060
However, these were considered worst-case scenario reports,

501
00:37:35,580 --> 00:37:39,000
and their managers concluded the strike was not serious enough to pose a danger.

502
00:37:41,080 --> 00:37:46,500
In footage recovered after Columbia's destruction, the crew appeared to relish their time in space.

503
00:37:46,501 --> 00:37:50,560
Good morning, Ken. Good morning, Paul.

504
00:37:50,561 --> 00:37:57,560
and a special good morning to my wife Rona, the love of my life.

505
00:37:57,561 --> 00:37:59,560
Whoo!

506
00:38:03,560 --> 00:38:05,560
Baseball.

507
00:38:05,561 --> 00:38:07,560
Hey, better, better, better, better, better, better.

508
00:38:07,561 --> 00:38:10,560
Zero G baseball.

509
00:38:10,561 --> 00:38:13,560
You're supposed to catch it, Eli.

510
00:38:15,560 --> 00:38:18,560
Bicycle kick.

511
00:38:18,561 --> 00:38:23,320
Wow, Willie, that was impressive.

512
00:38:23,321 --> 00:38:28,320
On the morning of February 1, 2003, the crew prepared to return home.

513
00:38:28,321 --> 00:38:32,320
That might be some plasma now.

514
00:38:32,321 --> 00:38:34,900
We see it out the front also.

515
00:38:34,901 --> 00:38:37,020
That's some plasma.

516
00:38:37,021 --> 00:38:42,460
A little north of Hawaii, at an altitude of 75 miles, Columbia penetrated the outer

517
00:38:42,461 --> 00:38:44,800
fringes of the atmosphere.

518
00:38:44,801 --> 00:38:49,680
After the afternoon, an orange glow surrounded the orbiter, as atmospheric friction began

519
00:38:49,681 --> 00:38:51,800
heating up the thermal protection system.

520
00:38:51,801 --> 00:38:54,800
This is amazing, it's really getting fairly bright out there.

521
00:38:54,801 --> 00:38:55,800
Yep.

522
00:38:55,801 --> 00:38:58,800
Yeah, you definitely don't want to be outside now.

523
00:38:58,801 --> 00:39:01,800
Looks like a blast to us.

524
00:39:01,801 --> 00:39:07,480
Twenty-three minutes prior to its scheduled landing in Florida, Columbia sailed just north

525
00:39:07,481 --> 00:39:12,800
of San Francisco, when mission control monitors began showing the first hints of trouble.

526
00:39:12,801 --> 00:39:20,220
FYI, I've just lost four separate temperature transducers on the left side of the vehicle.

527
00:39:20,880 --> 00:39:24,160
There was a halt in the flow of temperature readings from the left wing.

528
00:39:25,800 --> 00:39:28,820
Since sensor glitches during re-entry were not uncommon,

529
00:39:29,760 --> 00:39:32,760
Flight Director Leroy Kane was not yet overly concerned.

530
00:39:34,960 --> 00:39:38,620
Over the next five minutes, however, more sensors went dead,

531
00:39:38,621 --> 00:39:42,200
and the temperature in the left landing gear spiked 60 degrees.

532
00:39:42,800 --> 00:39:49,800
Tire pressure on left outboard and left inboard both tires.

533
00:39:49,801 --> 00:39:53,800
And Columbia, Houston, we see your tire pressure messages and we did not copy your last.

534
00:39:53,801 --> 00:39:55,800
Is it instrumentation, Max?

535
00:39:55,801 --> 00:39:58,800
Flight Max is also off-road.

536
00:40:03,800 --> 00:40:06,800
Communication with the crew was suddenly lost.

537
00:40:06,801 --> 00:40:12,880
Columbia was 39 miles over north-central Texas, traveling 18 times the speed of sound,

538
00:40:12,881 --> 00:40:15,320
16 minutes from its scheduled landing.

539
00:40:17,080 --> 00:40:25,360
While flight controllers tried to re-establish communication for several minutes,

540
00:40:26,460 --> 00:40:30,560
Texas and Louisiana residents were reporting mysterious loud noises

541
00:40:30,561 --> 00:40:33,620
and fireballs streaking through the sky.

542
00:40:33,621 --> 00:40:37,260
GC flight. GC flight.

543
00:40:37,920 --> 00:40:39,440
Flight GC. Lock the doors.

544
00:40:39,820 --> 00:40:40,520
Copy.

545
00:40:41,780 --> 00:40:47,000
At 9.29 a.m., Flight Director Kane declared a contingency,

546
00:40:47,880 --> 00:40:50,180
a NASA term meaning the shuttle had been lost.

547
00:40:51,000 --> 00:40:52,520
Mission control was locked down.

548
00:40:53,160 --> 00:40:55,280
Phone calls were not allowed in or out

549
00:40:55,281 --> 00:40:59,460
as controllers began saving and downloading all of Columbia's flight data.

550
00:40:59,461 --> 00:41:10,740
within minutes a massive recovery effort was begun it covered a 2 800 square mile area from mid Texas

551
00:41:10,741 --> 00:41:18,360
through Louisiana over the next several months the Columbia accident investigation board would

552
00:41:18,361 --> 00:41:25,220
conclude that superheated gases entered a breach in Columbia's left wing during re-entry and melted

553
00:41:25,221 --> 00:41:29,960
its internal aluminum structure. They blamed the breach on the foam strike

554
00:41:29,961 --> 00:41:36,440
during liftoff. The board conducted tests by shooting a piece of foam from a

555
00:41:36,441 --> 00:41:43,820
nitrogen-powered cannon at nearly 500 miles per hour directly at a carbon

556
00:41:43,821 --> 00:41:49,440
carbon panel. Even though the foam struck a glancing blow, it produced a

557
00:41:49,441 --> 00:41:56,440
hole 16 inches across, more than large enough to cause the breach.

558
00:41:56,441 --> 00:41:59,766
The board also made several recommendations.

559
00:41:59,767 --> 00:42:02,940
It called for modifications to the foam on

560
00:42:02,941 --> 00:42:06,042
the external tank to reduce debris strikes.

561
00:42:06,043 --> 00:42:09,360
It also recommended that astronauts be trained

562
00:42:09,361 --> 00:42:14,560
to repair the thermal protection system in orbit and that better photographic coverage

563
00:42:14,561 --> 00:42:16,960
be provided between launch and ascent.

564
00:42:18,540 --> 00:42:22,460
The remaining space shuttles are expected to fly for at least another decade,

565
00:42:23,120 --> 00:42:26,340
as they continue to play a vital role for the International Space Station.

566
00:42:28,020 --> 00:42:30,800
While various replacements for the shuttle are under study,

567
00:42:31,640 --> 00:42:34,220
none are expected to come online in the near future.

568
00:42:38,020 --> 00:42:40,320
In the days following the disaster,

569
00:42:41,120 --> 00:42:44,720
A memorial service was held on the shuttle runway at Kennedy Space Center.

570
00:42:46,260 --> 00:42:50,400
Bob Crippen, Columbia's first pilot, talked about his old friend.

571
00:42:50,860 --> 00:42:54,560
I'm sure that Columbia, which had traveled millions of miles

572
00:42:54,561 --> 00:42:59,060
and made that fiery reentry 27 times before,

573
00:43:00,460 --> 00:43:06,240
struggled mightily in those last moments to bring her crew home safely once again.

574
00:43:07,560 --> 00:43:09,160
She wasn't successful.

575
00:43:10,320 --> 00:43:17,740
Columbia and the other orbiters are all named after great explorer ships for

576
00:43:17,741 --> 00:43:22,980
that is their mission to explore the unknown. Columbia was hardly a thing of

577
00:43:22,981 --> 00:43:28,440
beauty except those of us who loved and cared for. Many said she was old and

578
00:43:28,441 --> 00:43:34,680
passed her prime. Still she had only lived barely a quarter of her design

579
00:43:34,681 --> 00:43:38,941
life. Columbia had a great many missions ahead of her.

580
00:43:38,942 --> 00:43:41,940
She, along with the crew, had her life

581
00:43:41,941 --> 00:43:43,796
snuffed out in her prime.

582
00:43:43,797 --> 00:43:48,620
There's heavy grief in our hearts, which will diminish with time,

583
00:43:48,621 --> 00:43:53,700
but it will never go away, and we will never forget.

584
00:43:53,701 --> 00:43:57,560
Hail Rick

585
00:43:57,561 --> 00:43:58,980
Willie

586
00:43:58,981 --> 00:44:00,380
Casey

587
00:44:00,381 --> 00:44:01,740
Mike

588
00:44:01,741 --> 00:44:03,380
Laurel

589
00:44:03,381 --> 00:44:04,860
Dave

590
00:44:04,861 --> 00:44:05,680
and Alain

591
00:44:05,681 --> 00:44:07,700
Hail Columbia

